  "vegSub": "Regional Ecosystem 12.5.3 · Eucalyptus racemosa woodland on remnant Tertiary surfaces (Endangered) · Southeast Queensland",
  "intro": "Before 1750 your address stood in tall, shrubby woodland of Scribbly Gum, Pink Bloodwood and Grey Ironbark, often with White Stringybark, Blackbutt and Tallowwood, on the old iron-rich lateritic soils of coastal Southeast Queensland. Of roughly 57,000 hectares that once grew from Noosa southward, about 6,000 remain — most of it cleared for pine plantation, horticulture and suburbs — and it is now listed as Endangered. It is prime koala country and a hollow-rich home for gliders, possums and birds, so replanting it here genuinely matters. The plants below are indigenous to this community and grouped by the layer they fill. Plant the canopy first; the shrubs, lilies, sedges and grasses belong in the light beneath it.",
